WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.430
Part 2 of nine: Notice barriers and ask without assumptions.

2
00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.040
The standards connected with this part are 1, 4, 12.

3
00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:13.710
Here is the work this part helps you prepare: Separate

4
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:17.050
an observation from an assumption; write a respectful question

5
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.500
Begin with what you know.

6
00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:23.310
I have an example. How do I describe a problem

7
00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:26.190
without quietly turning my first guess into a fact?

8
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.900
In the first lesson, you chose a work example and

9
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.520
described what someone needs to do with it. Now we

10
00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.940
will examine what might get in the way. This lesson

11
00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.500
connects the principal standard with ongoing workforce learning and understanding

12
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:39.400
community needs.

13
00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:43.430
Start with an observation you could describe without assigning a

14
00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:47.110
motive. For our sample notice, the observation is that some

15
00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:49.450
people have not responded. Saying that they do not care

16
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.610
is an interpretation. Saying that they cannot understand the notice

17
00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.130
is also an interpretation until we have more information.

18
00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:00.080
A respectful inquiry begins by acknowledging what we do not

19
00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.140
know. We can say, where our role permits contact, that

20
00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.560
we want to check whether the person received the information

21
00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.980
and whether another way of explaining or responding would help.

22
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.340
We do not need to begin by asking the person

23
00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:13.260
to defend their behavior.

24
00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:15.770
Look at the whole encounter.

25
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:19.980
Could we follow the steps from receiving the notice to

26
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.300
responding, instead of looking only at the final response count?

27
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:28.250
Imagine the steps involved in responding. The person must receive

28
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:33.010
the notice, recognize its purpose, understand the options, decide what

29
00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.690
they want to do, and use an available response method.

30
00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.750
A difficulty at any one step can look like a

31
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:40.130
lack of interest from the other end.

32
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.160
Possible barriers include unfamiliar terminology, information in a format someone

33
00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.980
cannot access, no interpreter when needed, an inconvenient appointment, transportation,

34
00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:55.540
unreliable technology, or previous experiences that make the process difficult

35
00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:56.080
to trust.

36
00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:01.460
These are possibilities to investigate, not explanations to assign automatically.

37
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.470
Also look for strengths and supports. A person may already

38
00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.670
have an effective communication method, a trusted support person they

39
00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.650
choose, or a clear preference about how to receive information.

40
00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:15.350
A community may have organizations with relevant knowledge and established

41
00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:19.150
relationships. Understanding assets as well as barriers is part of

42
00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:21.330
the community assessment described in standard twelve.

43
00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.320
Culture is a starting point for curiosity.

44
00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:29.110
Where does cultural context help us, and where could a

45
00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:30.490
generalization get in the way?

46
00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:36.260
Culture can shape communication, expectations and experiences. It does not

47
00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:39.980
determine every person's preferences. People within the same community differ,

48
00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.280
and a person's preferences can change with the situation.

49
00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:46.970
Avoid turning general information about a community into a rule

50
00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.910
about an individual. Instead of deciding that a family will

51
00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.730
want a particular person to speak, ask whom the person

52
00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.630
would like involved and how they want to participate. Instead

53
00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.810
of interpreting silence as agreement, offer time and a way

54
00:02:58.810 --> 00:02:59.390
to ask questions.

55
00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:03.610
Instead of treating an accent as evidence of understanding or

56
00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.470
misunderstanding, check the clarity of the information itself.

57
00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:11.180
Cultural humility involves examining your own assumptions as well. Familiar

58
00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.600
procedures can feel straightforward to the people who use them

59
00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.880
every day. They may be entirely unfamiliar to someone encountering

60
00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.600
them for the first time. Recognizing that difference is an

61
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.100
opportunity to improve the explanation.

62
00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:23.930
Practice a useful question.

63
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.140
What would a more useful opening question sound like?

64
00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:32.210
Consider these two openings. The first asks, why have you

65
00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:34.910
failed to return the form? The second explains the purpose

66
00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:37.870
of the form, asks whether the person received it, and

67
00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:41.530
offers help identifying a usable way to respond. The second

68
00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:44.650
opening provides information and creates room for the person's explanation.

69
00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.680
That does not mean ignoring a deadline or a required

70
00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.740
step. It means explaining the requirement clearly while asking what

71
00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.900
support is needed. Do not promise an extension or exception

72
00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.260
that you cannot authorize. If an adjustment needs approval, explain

73
00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.400
what you can do next and who will review it.

74
00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:05.250
In the practice activity, separate what was observed from what

75
00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.170
was assumed. Then choose a question that invites the person's

76
00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:11.410
perspective without requiring private information that is not needed for

77
00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:11.730
the task.

78
00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:14.880
Apply inquiry to your own role.

79
00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.970
What if my role is behind the scenes, or I

80
00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:20.650
supervise the people doing this work?

81
00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.740
You may not have direct contact with people receiving services.

82
00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:28.580
If you work on policy, data, contracts or communications, inquiry

83
00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:32.080
still matters. Ask how the process was tested, whose experience

84
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.300
informed it, which access needs were considered, and where feedback

85
00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:39.160
is recorded. Consult the appropriate people rather than inventing an

86
00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.660
answer or contacting someone outside your responsibilities.

87
00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:46.730
A supervisor can help staff discuss uncertainty without shaming them

88
00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:47.730
for asking questions.

89
00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:51.590
A manager can look for repeated barriers across encounters.

90
00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:55.770
A director can ask whether resources and accountability support the

91
00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:56.090
response.

92
00:04:56.470 --> 00:05:00.810
Regular learning, addressed in standard four, should help people practice

93
00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:04.050
these decisions rather than merely acknowledge that they read a

94
00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:04.290
policy.

95
00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.040
Add to your working example.

96
00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.190
Let's turn that into an inquiry I can actually make

97
00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:11.990
within my role.

98
00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:14.780
Return to your selected work example.

99
00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:18.900
Write one observation, one assumption you need to set aside,

100
00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.640
and one respectful question that would help you learn more.

101
00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.460
Then identify an appropriate way to obtain that information within

102
00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:25.900
your role.

103
00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:29.230
If there is no direct access to the people affected,

104
00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:32.170
do not fill the gap by guessing. Name the gap

105
00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:36.430
and identify who can help. Existing feedback, approved engagement processes,

106
00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:40.050
accessibility reviews or a discussion with the responsible program area

107
00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.550
may provide a starting point.

108
00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.780
The next lesson follows a language-assistance request through an encounter.

109
00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.960
Keep the habit from this lesson with you: ask about

110
00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.920
needs and preferences, describe what you know, and leave room

111
00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.280
for information that could change your first interpretation.

112
00:05:56.210 --> 00:06:00.050
Let's work through the companion example: Replace a conclusion with

113
00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:00.450
a question

114
00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.780
Here is the situation. A colleague says, 'People who do

115
00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:08.640
not reply are not interested.' The available information only shows

116
00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.100
that a reply was not received.

117
00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:13.670
What response would move this example forward responsibly?

118
00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:19.480
Ask what is known about delivery, understanding, timing and available

119
00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:24.240
response methods. Where contact is appropriate, ask whether the person

120
00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.220
received the notice and whether another explanation or response method

121
00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:28.640
would help.

122
00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:32.570
And here is why that response fits. The question leaves

123
00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:35.750
room for the person's explanation and avoids treating an assumption

124
00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:36.370
as evidence.

125
00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:40.420
Now make your own choice. A person has missed two

126
00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:43.520
appointments. Which note best separates observation from assumption?

127
00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:47.870
Option 1. They missed two appointments, so they do not

128
00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:48.690
value the service.

129
00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:53.200
Option 2. They missed two appointments because people from their

130
00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:54.720
community prefer another process.

131
00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:58.890
Option 3. Two appointments were missed. We need to learn

132
00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:02.470
whether the information, timing, communication or another part of the

133
00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:03.950
process was difficult to use.

134
00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:06.920
Pause the player now if you want to choose and

135
00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.620
explain your reasoning. You can use your own notes or

136
00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:12.660
the companion activity. When you're ready, resume and we'll work

137
00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:13.840
through all three responses.

138
00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:16.430
How should we think about option 1?

139
00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:21.000
The first part is an observation; the conclusion assigns a

140
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.120
motive without evidence.

141
00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:25.270
How should we think about option 2?

142
00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:30.260
A generalization about a community does not explain this person's

143
00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:34.100
experience. Ask rather than assign a cultural explanation.

144
00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:37.410
How should we think about option 3?

145
00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:42.760
This describes what is known and identifies questions. Use the

146
00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:46.980
appropriate contact or review process to learn more, without collecting

147
00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:48.420
unnecessary private information.

148
00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:52.530
Option 3 is the supported choice in this activity.

149
00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:56.040
You can choose another response and compare the reasoning.

150
00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:01.090
Write one observation, one assumption to set aside and one

151
00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.810
respectful question. How could you obtain the answer within your

152
00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:04.030
role?

153
00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:08.600
The working note from this part is: Observation and inquiry

154
00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:11.990
Pause the player if you want time to make that

155
00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:16.510
note. Keep it general, without names or private details. Thinking

156
00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:18.730
it through without writing is also fine.

157
00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:23.400
That completes part 2. You can return to any passage

158
00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:26.720
or take a break. The next part is Make language

159
00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:27.680
assistance usable.
